    {
      type: "paragraph",
      text: "This trip, as all last-minute trips are wont to be, came at a really busy time in our professional lives. This meant that we both had our laptops in arms reach throughout our flight and trains, ready to seize the chance of stable wifi. After landing, we immediately took a Shinkansen and then Kintetsu to Mie Prefecture.",
    },
    {
      type: "image",
      src: "/202603/japan-train.png",
      alt: "Japan train",
      caption: "We traversed quite a long distance in one day to get closer to Suzuka Circuit for F1.",
    },
    {
      type: "paragraph",
      text: "Unfortunately, there was a slight delay at the Tokyo station due to \"flying debris\", which I assumed to be a suicide attempt. The 15-minute delay unfortunately led us to miss the rest of our connections, but we snagged the last Kintetsu train. Once we arrived in Iseshi, I finally stowed my laptop away, tethered to my unreliable unlimited hotspot. We spent a day eating Ise-ebi (gigantic shrimp) and Matsusaka beef, and the next day at the Suzuka Circuit, where fans were very subded for two reasons: Yuki Tsunoda was no longer racing for Red Bull, and Honda x Aston Martin partnership showed subpar performance. Overall, I would recommend Japan F1 season because of the ease of travel if not the price.",
    },
    {
      type: "paragraph",
      text: "After the race was over, we spent a couple days in Kyoto, looking at sakura and eating our way through town. I was concerned that all the sakura would be blown away from the wind and rain, but I was availed of my concerns and was able to take many photos of my Grand Seiko Hanami amongst all the sakura. As for the mocktail scene in Japan, I must say that they were not as creative as their cocktails, and could/would not produce anything that strayed away from their menus. Granted, my sample size of 3 was not representative of the mocktail scene, however I would expect a lot more from those in the list for The World's 50 Best Bars."
    },
    {type: "paragraph",
      text: "Finally, I am really proud to report that I am now a pro at packing light for international trips: no suitcases, just one backpack and a zip tote bag. I was rather tired of my outfits after a week, but it was worth not having to drag heavy cases up and down staircases, especially as we took multiple trains around the country.",
